hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're going to talk  about the two different types of  speciation that is allopatric speciation  and sympatric speciation but first let's  talk about speciation itself speciation  is just the origin of a new species and  in most cases it leads to or is a result  of reproductive isolation that is one of  the things that makes them a distinct  species is the inability to reproduce  with members of another species now  let's talk about allopatric speciation  allopatric comes from the greek for  different homeland and that's a clue in  allopatric speciation gene flow is  interrupted by geographic isolation gene  flow simply means the flow of genetic  information between individuals so for  example the Galapagos finches and other  animals that are on the Galapagos  Islands are a good example with  allopatric speciation this is because  most of them are related to other  species on the mainland meaning that  they shared once upon a time a common  ancestor with this other species but  they've been geographically isolated at  one point one of their ancestors made it  from the mainland to one of the islands  in the Galapagos and mutations were  acquired and various physical  characteristics were changed over time  that led to the census being different  species after being geographically  isolated from the parent species another  example of this is squirrels that live  on the north or south side of the Grand  Canyon you can imagine that two  squirrels the Grand Canyon presents a  major geographic isolation the squirrels  are too small to get from one side of  the canyon to another and so over time  after the canyon form you have the  squirrels on the North Face of the  canyon and the squirrels on the South  face of the canyon developing into  different species let's think about what  this might look like in a pictorial way  here the black represents two different  geographic habitats so we've got animals  that are separated by a geographic  isolation event perhaps it is plate  tectonics creating a mountain range  perhaps it is water levels and a lake  changing so that a large lake is now  made is now sunk into a few different  distinct ponds but what can happen there  is you have individuals in one of the  habitats developing into one species and  individuals and the other habitat  developing into a different species as a  result of the geographic isolation so  that is allopatric speciation in  sympatric speciation the chain flow  between individuals is reduced by some  other means so not geographic isolation  but the gene flow is reduced in  geographically overlapping populations  for example if you have a habitat where  two different species are forming from a  parent species it would look something  like this  where the two species are forming  despite being in a geographically  overlapping range examples of this in  plants include different types of  polyploidy remember that polyploidy is  rare and animals with pretty common  plants and it is a result of mistakes  during cell division that result in the  daughter plants having extra sets of  chromosomes so if you have two plants  that develop extra sets of chromosomes  around the same time they can mate with  each other leading to plant offspring  that also have you different  of chromosomes and these can result in  different species within the same  geographic location another example of  sexual selection in animals  so for example there are certain insects  that prefer to live on a certain type of  tree and this may allow them to diverge  as a new species from insects that  prefer to live on a different type of  tree for example insects that prefer to  eat from a certain type of flower and  then they're more likely to reproduce at  that flower this can eventually lead to  a different type of species forming  because of this type of sexual selection  also things like coloration patterns  whenever you have females of the species  that prefer males that have a certain  coloration pattern made birds and fish  have this kind of sexual selection this  over time can lead to different species  despite the fact that the individual  populations are overlapping so this is  the difference in allopatric speciation  which means different homeland and  sympatric speciation meaning same  homeland but they are both ways that a  new species can form and they both  involve some kind of reproductive  isolation instead patryk speciation the  reproductive isolation happens first and  this allows the new species to form in  allopatric speciation  there's the geographic isolation first  and then over time because of various  mutations the reproductive isolation can  follow so that is it for this episode of  biology professor thanks for watching I  hope you learned a lot
 

